##### **1.6 Direction Fields and Tangent Slope Geometry**

For a first-order ordinary differential equation solved for the derivative,
$$\frac{dx}{dt} = f(t, x), \tag{1.1}$$
the derivative $\frac{dx}{dt}$ represents the slope of the tangent line to the solution trajectory $x(t)$ at the point $(t, x)$. The function $f(t, x)$ assigns to each coordinate pair $(t^*, x^*)$ in its domain a definite tangent slope $k = f(t^*, x^*)$ without requiring analytical integration of the equation.

A **direction field** (or **slope field**) is a geometric representation of equation (1.1) consisting of short line segments of uniform length $L$ centered at discrete grid points $(t_i, x_j)$ across a region in the $(t, x)$-plane, where the slope of each segment equals $k_{ij} = f(t_i, x_j)$. Under standard existence and uniqueness conditions, exactly one smooth integral curve passes through any chosen point $(t^*, x^*)$, remaining tangent to the field segments at every point along its trajectory.

