The construction of a direction field follows a systematic visual and numerical algorithm:

1. Specify a bounded rectangular domain $[t_{\min}, t_{\max}] \times [x_{\min}, x_{\max}]$.
2. Discretize the domain into a grid of coordinates $(t_i, x_j)$ with regular grid spacings $\Delta t = t_{i+1} - t_i$ and $\Delta x = x_{j+1} - x_j$.
3. Choose a fixed segment length $L$ that satisfies the geometric constraint $L < \min(\Delta t, \Delta x)$, ensuring adjacent segments cannot overlap or cross one another.
4. Evaluate the slope $k_{ij} = f(t_i, x_j)$ at each node $(t_i, x_j)$.
5. Determine the line passing through $(t_i, x_j)$ with slope $k_{ij}$:
   $$x - x_j = k_{ij}(t - t_i). \tag{1.2}$$
6. Plot a segment of length $L$ along this line, centered at $(t_i, x_j)$, bounded by the endpoints $\left(t_i \pm \frac{L}{2\sqrt{1 + k_{ij}^2}}, \; x_j \pm \frac{k_{ij}L}{2\sqrt{1 + k_{ij}^2}}\right)$.
7. Repeat the evaluation and plotting across all grid points.

Consider the equation $\frac{dx}{dt} = 1$. The right-hand side is constant, yielding $k_{ij} = f(t_i, x_j) = 1$ across all coordinates. On a grid with step sizes $\Delta t = 1$ and $\Delta x = 1$, setting segment length $L = 0.5$ satisfies $L < \min(\Delta t, \Delta x)$. At each node, the segment lies on $x - x_j = t - t_i$ inclined at an angle of $\arctan(1) = 45^\circ$, forming a uniform field of parallel segments that trace the integral curves $x(t) = t + C$.
